{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>What to actually measure instead of the percentage</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Treat a coverage percentage as a checklist of what's untested, not a score of how well-tested something is. Zero coverage on a file is a real, useful signal, nobody's looked at it at all. 100% coverage is not the mirror-image good signal it looks like; it just means every line ran at least once under some test, with no guarantee about what those tests actually verified.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Tools like Stryker Mutator (JS/TS) run mutation testing directly against your existing suite: install it, point it at the same files your coverage tool already reports on, and read the mutation score, the percentage of injected bugs your tests actually caught, alongside (not instead of) your line coverage number. A file with 100% line coverage and a 40% mutation score is a much more honest signal that something's wrong than either number alone.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      For code where full mutation testing isn't practical (slow suites, flaky infra, tight deadlines), a lightweight substitute is a deliberate risk-review question added to code review itself: for any code touching money, auth, or data integrity, ask "what's the worst realistic thing this could silently get wrong," and require the PR to point at a test asserting against exactly that scenario, not just that the new lines are exercised. This catches what percentage metrics structurally can't, because the risk lives in whether the test's inputs and mocks represent the dangerous real-world scenario, not in which lines execute.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

