{# ── Chapter: Takeaway ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="takeaway">
  <h2>Remember</h2>
  <div class="case-takeaway">
    <h3>Key takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <div class="tone-junior">100% line coverage only means every line ran during tests — it doesn't mean the tests checked anything correct about what should have happened, a test with a weak assertion can hit every line and still miss the real bug.</div>
        <div class="tone-mid">Mutation testing asks a different question than coverage: if this code were deliberately broken, would any test notice? A suite that lets an injected bug survive is a stronger warning sign than any coverage percentage.</div>
        <div class="tone-staff">Coverage can't distinguish a mock scenario from the real risky scenario it's meant to represent — pair mutation testing where practical with a risk-weighted review question ("what's the worst realistic thing this silently gets wrong") for code touching money, auth, or data integrity.</div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
